<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: #ffffff; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Exploring My Community</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's the Park!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Comprehend simple descriptions of people, places, and things around them. Interactive: Answer simple questions about a story they have just listened to.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can demonstrate understanding of basic descriptions of people, places, and things around them (e.g., pointing to the park when listening "The park is next to the school."). Can answer simple questions about familiar stories just heard (e.g., "Where is the boy in the story?" "At the market."). Can identify simple characteristics of people and places in basic oral conversations or descriptions (e.g., "It's a big park." "Maria is tall and Jorge is short.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; text-align: left; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; text-align: left; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; text-align: left; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b><br>Students are engaged, prior knowledge is activated, and new language is introduced and modeled by the teacher.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students with a "Hello Song." Then, the teacher shows large flashcards of community places (park, market, school, library, church, store) one by one. Students repeat the names chorally. The teacher plays a short, simple song about visiting community places, encouraging students to point to the corresponding flashcard when they hear the word. (e.g., "Let's go to the park!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up flashcards of "park" and "market." The teacher models the pronunciation of "park" /pɑːrk/ and "market" /ˈmɑːrkɪt/ clearly, exaggerating the initial /p/ and /m/ sounds. Students listen and repeat chorally. The teacher then introduces "big" and "small" using objects or by pointing to different sized flashcards/objects, modeling "It's a big park." and "It's a small store." Students repeat. The teacher also models polite expressions like "Excuse me, where is the park?" and "Thank you."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up the park flashcard) "Is this a school?" (Students shake heads/say "No.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the park) "Where do we play?" (Students point to the park or say "Park!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">__/__<br>8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>New language input is presented through an authentic context, focusing on listening for global and specific comprehension.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Exploring My Community":</b> The teacher introduces a simple audio story or animated video (e.g., a "Peppa Pig" short clip) about a child, Lily, visiting different places in her community. The story is kept simple and repetitive, using the target vocabulary and grammar. Example phrases: "This is Lily. Lily goes to the park. The park is big. She plays on the playground. Then, Lily goes to the market. The market is busy. She sees many things."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio story or animated video clip (narrative excerpt), picture flashcards of "park," "market," "school," "playground," "big," "small."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Comprehend simple descriptions of people, places, and things around them.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions (First listening):</b> "Who is in the story?" (Lily) "Where does Lily go?" (Students point to pictures of the park/market).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Second listening):</b> The teacher presents simple statements while students listen again. "Lily goes to the school." (Students show 'false' gesture or point to wrong picture). "The park is big." (Students show 'true' gesture or point to big park picture).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching (Third listening):</b> Teacher says a place, students point to the corresponding characteristic from a choice of two pictures (e.g., "The park is..." (students point to 'big' or 'small' picture)).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">__/__<br>10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>Activities designed to practice the new language with a focus on accuracy in listening comprehension.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> WH-questions ("Where is it?"), Present simple ("It's the market."), Adjectives + nouns ("big park").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> park, market, school, library, church, store, playground, big, small, go, play, see.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Initial /p/ in "park", /m/ in "market".</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled practice (Listen and Point - TPR):</b> The teacher calls out a place or a description (e.g., "Point to the school!", "Point to the big park!"). Students listen and physically point to the correct flashcard or picture on their desk. The teacher ensures correct pronunciation and prompts students to echo single words or short phrases as they point. (Focus on "park" /p/ and "market" /m/).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled practice (Listen and Circle/Match):</b> Students receive a worksheet with pictures of different community places (park, market, school, church, library) and some simple adjectives (big, small) next to them. The teacher reads descriptions aloud: "Circle the small church." "Match the big market." "Draw a line to the library." Students listen carefully and perform the action on their worksheet.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction / Peer Check (Listen and Find):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair has a set of flashcards with community places. One student (A) listens while the teacher describes a place using simple sentences (e.g., "It is a big place. Children play there. You can run there."). Student A listens and finds the correct flashcard ("park"). Student A then shows it to Student B for confirmation. The teacher monitors and helps with vocabulary and comprehension.</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">__/__<br>12-15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>Students use the target language in a more realistic, communicative task, focusing on fluency and task completion.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task: "Listen and Color My Community Map"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students receive a simple black and white drawing of a community map with outlines of a "park," "school," "market," "church," and "home."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives clear, simple listening instructions. (e.g., "Listen carefully!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The park is big. Color the park green."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The school is red. Color the school red."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The market is yellow. Color the market yellow."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The church is blue. Color the church blue."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"My home is small. Color your home orange."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This task requires students to actively listen to descriptions and identify the places and their characteristics (colors, big/small) to complete the coloring activity. It allows for individual production based on listening comprehension.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">__/__<br>8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>Formative assessment of learning outcomes and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Listen and Color My Community Map" activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete the "Listen and Color My Community Map" worksheet. The teacher collects the colored maps.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can identify (point/color) the "park," "school," "market," "church," "home" based on oral descriptions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can identify "big" and "small" when associated with places.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Follows multi-step oral instructions (e.g., "Color the park green.").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Before leaving, students verbally answer (or point to a smiley/frowning face):</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you understand the names of the places today?" (Yes/No or point to faces)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you hear the /p/ in 'park'?" (Thumbs up/down)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">__/__<br>5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b><br>Students reflect on their learning, and the teacher considers the lesson's effectiveness and future adjustments.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new place did you hear about today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is your favorite place in the community?" (Students can point to a picture or say a single word).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson? (e.g., TPR activities, visual aids, clarity of instructions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What challenges did students face regarding listening comprehension? (e.g., distinguishing between words, understanding adjectives).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension)? (e.g., more practice with specific vocabulary, slower audio, re-emphasize "next to" for spatial relationships).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In the next lesson, students will practice identifying these same community places and adjectives in short written texts, building on the listening comprehension established today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">__/__<br>2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>